lesson:
C# (said "C sharp") is Microsoft's main language: Windows apps, web servers, and above all **Unity**, the engine behind a huge share of indie and mobile games. If you know a little Java, C# will feel familiar.

```
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, world!");
        Console.Write("Same line... ");
        Console.WriteLine(2 + 3);
    }
}
```

- `using System;` lets you write `Console` instead of `System.Console`.
- The program starts in `Main`. `Console.WriteLine` prints a line; `Console.Write` doesn't end it.
- Statements end with `;`; braces `{ }` group code. C# style puts each brace on its own line.
- **String interpolation** mixes text and values: `Console.WriteLine($"Total: {40 + 2}");` (note the `$`).

C# is compiled: `dotnet build` checks the whole program first, and `dotnet run` builds and runs it. Archie builds your exercises in a scratch project, so you only write the `.cs` file. (Newer C# can skip the class and Main for tiny programs; this course writes them out, because Unity scripts and real projects look like this.)

example.code:
using System;

class Program
{
    static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello, world!");
        Console.Write("Same line... ");
        Console.WriteLine("now a new one.");
        Console.WriteLine($"Total: {40 + 2}");
        Console.WriteLine("4" + "2");
    }
}

